1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,200
War. What is it good for?

2
00:00:04,201 --> 00:00:07,200
Pain? Suffering? Death?

3
00:00:07,201 --> 00:00:15,700
But perhaps technological leaps affecting everything from the way we work to the vehicles we drive to the food we eat.

4
00:00:15,701 --> 00:00:20,200
Now, Inventions of War on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,700
War is never good.

6
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:47,700
In the 20th century, tens of millions of people lost their lives.

7
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:53,140
Ironically, amidst the carnage, disease and pain of war,

8
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:56,220
have emerged some of history's greatest inventions,

9
00:00:57,080 --> 00:00:59,760
including revolutionary developments in communication.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,300
transportation, and medicine.

11
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:09,100
So many of our everyday items were developed during war

12
00:01:09,101 --> 00:01:13,480
that it leaves people wondering about the controversial role war plays

13
00:01:13,481 --> 00:01:15,680
in a society's technological advances.

14
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:20,320
Wars serve as a presser function, an accelerator,

15
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:25,360
to bring things from research to utilization much more quickly.

16
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:29,340
Technology tends to move forward in wartime

17
00:01:29,341 --> 00:01:32,940
because you're in the ultimate competition.

18
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:36,600
If the other guy competes and you don't, you die.

19
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:39,820
That's a strong incentive to compete.

20
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:43,540
Funds that would otherwise not be available become available.

21
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:45,420
People stop worrying about the budget

22
00:01:45,421 --> 00:01:49,300
because the outcome is more important than the cost.

23
00:01:49,301 --> 00:01:51,660
The whole society becomes mobilized.

24
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:57,240
One of the most remarkable things

25
00:01:57,241 --> 00:01:59,120
is how much of what we take for granted

26
00:01:59,121 --> 00:02:03,300
in our everyday life came out of top secret military developments

27
00:02:03,301 --> 00:02:06,240
that were often war-winning technologies.

28
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:11,960
Yes, wars have given us products we use on a day-to-day basis

29
00:02:11,961 --> 00:02:15,960
that we're not even aware of, like plastics.

30
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:19,300
It would be hard to imagine life today

31
00:02:19,301 --> 00:02:22,020
without these carbon-based synthetic materials.

32
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:25,980
Molded into countless shapes and objects,

33
00:02:25,981 --> 00:02:30,160
The plastics industry is a $300 billion a year business.

34
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:37,340
Scientists actually invented several of the modern thermoplastics just prior to World War II.

35
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:42,260
Some plastics sat on the shelf, waiting to find a purpose for their existence.

36
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:46,260
Others, such as nylon, found an immediate market.

37
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:54,500
DuPont unveiled the miracle fiber nylon to an enthusiastic crowd at the 1939 World's Fair.

38
00:02:55,980 --> 00:03:00,500
The DuPont representative declared nylon to be stronger than steel.

39
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:08,560
Although the demand for nylon stockings was high, people had yet to realize the true potential of plastics.

40
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:14,560
But as war broke out, the miracle material was about to give the Allies a leg up.

41
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:22,820
The war effort sent chemists back into the lab and back into the vault to find new uses for plastics.

42
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:33,560
In the early 1940s, researchers discovered that plastic, such as nylon, could be molded into a variety of shapes.

43
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:40,740
So suddenly, if you could release a pound of steel, a pound of aluminum, replace it with nylon,

44
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:44,320
you could build another tank, you could build another gun,

45
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:48,240
you could build something which had to be made of a strategic metal.

46
00:03:48,241 --> 00:03:57,280
plastic was looked to more and more as the war went on as a substitute for metal

47
00:03:57,281 --> 00:04:05,300
researchers found new uses for existing plastics manufacturers used nylon for parachutes

48
00:04:05,301 --> 00:04:12,540
parachute cord and airplane tires engineers substituted vinyl and polyethylene for rubber

49
00:04:12,541 --> 00:04:18,140
The plastics industry generated millions of tons of materials during the war.

50
00:04:19,340 --> 00:04:25,500
When the war ended, manufacturers introduced plastic items from hairbrushes to Tupperware,

51
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:28,000
ushering in the synthetic century.

52
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:41,980
Dispensing everything from paint to shaving cream, aerosol cans make life easier.

53
00:04:42,540 --> 00:04:49,100
The spray can utilizes a low-pressure propellant, but it was invented under high-pressure conditions.

54
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:55,000
That convenient aerosol container was originally designed to save lives.

55
00:04:55,940 --> 00:04:57,746
During World War II, U.S.

56
00:04:57,747 --> 00:05:04,680
troops in the Pacific battled an enemy that was almost as dangerous as the Japanese, mosquitoes.

57
00:05:04,681 --> 00:05:12,840
Mosquitoes transmitted malaria, eventually infecting 124,000 troops in the Pacific Theater.

58
00:05:14,680 --> 00:05:17,471
In 1942, Dr. Lyle Goodhue of the U.S.

59
00:05:17,472 --> 00:05:21,620
Department of Agriculture struggled to find a solution.

60
00:05:22,620 --> 00:05:28,500
He combined a propellant gas and insecticide in a metal cylinder, then attached a simple nozzle.

61
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:34,580
The first aerosol can was designed to kill insects, not body odor.

62
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:42,120
Before war's end, Goodhue left the Department of Agriculture and started his own company

63
00:05:42,121 --> 00:05:47,440
in Neotashay, Kansas, to make aerosol cans for the fight against mosquitoes.

64
00:05:47,441 --> 00:05:53,260
The Crown Court Conceal was a can manufacturer.

65
00:05:53,261 --> 00:06:01,720
They were approached with the idea of making an aerosol can, and what they had done was

66
00:06:01,721 --> 00:06:08,340
take a beer can, a beer can production line, and modified it to generate the first aerosol cans.

67
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:13,220
That can was called the spray tainer, a unique name for the product

68
00:06:13,221 --> 00:06:17,180
because it was a can that was used to spray product out, something that never happened before.

69
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,980
Soldiers used the aerosol company's cans to kill insects in tight quarters.

70
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:25,380
Malaria cases dropped dramatically.

71
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,560
Thanks in part to the cans known as bug bombs.

72
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:36,720
After the war, companies introduced every imaginable product in a can.

73
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:40,720
People fell in love with a spray can.

74
00:06:40,721 --> 00:06:50,720
But scientists discovered in the 1970s that CFC, chlorofluorocarbon propellants, contributed to the depletion of the ozone layer.

75
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:58,720
When CFCs reached the stratosphere, the molecule is broken down by ultraviolet radiation.

76
00:06:58,721 --> 00:07:03,720
This releases chlorine atoms, and chlorine is what destroys ozone.

77
00:07:03,721 --> 00:07:05,137
In 1978, the U.S.

78
00:07:05,138 --> 00:07:12,720
demanded replacement of aerosol CFC propellants, making them more environmentally friendly.

79
00:07:12,721 --> 00:07:17,720
Today, the majority of aerosol cans use hydrocarbon propellants.

80
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:23,720
War changed even the food we eat.

81
00:07:23,721 --> 00:07:27,720
Before preservatives, most foods wouldn't last more than a week.

82
00:07:28,340 --> 00:07:31,320
But one great war leader asked, can it?

83
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:34,360
Napoleon once said that armies moved on their stomach.

84
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:38,100
And if you look back over the history of warfare,

85
00:07:38,320 --> 00:07:42,780
the two things that have aborted more campaigns

86
00:07:42,781 --> 00:07:49,300
and destroyed more armies than any of the weaponry they faced

87
00:07:49,301 --> 00:07:53,560
were disease and failure of food supplies.

88
00:07:54,440 --> 00:07:58,220
Food spoilage on long military campaigns was such a problem

89
00:07:58,221 --> 00:08:03,580
that in 1795, the French government offered a 12,000 franc prize

90
00:08:03,581 --> 00:08:06,580
to anyone who discovered a new way to preserve food.

91
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:11,100
Until then, armies relied on pickling or drying.

92
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:15,060
Both altered the taste, and still the food spoiled.

93
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:19,660
A French chef, Nicolas Appert, accepted the challenge.

94
00:08:19,661 --> 00:08:28,060
After 14 years of experimentation, he discovered that by first sealing food in glass bottles,

95
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:33,200
then boiling the jars for several hours, shelf life increased dramatically.

96
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:38,580
In 1809, he won the award and began supplying food to the army.

97
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:43,940
A pair's method worked because boiling killed the bacteria within the containers.

98
00:08:43,941 --> 00:08:51,760
A year later, Peter Durand in England patented the first tin can, which held up better than glass bottles.

99
00:08:52,700 --> 00:08:55,240
The Royal Navy was soon buying tinned food.

100
00:09:01,140 --> 00:09:01,840
Meet Dr.

101
00:09:01,841 --> 00:09:06,960
Edwin Moore, one of the men who made orange juice synonymous with breakfast.

102
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:13,460
Well, as a kid, you had oranges perhaps at Thanksgiving or Christmas.

103
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:16,680
Later on, you had canned orange juice.

104
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:22,260
But I always thought, boy, you can always improve on canned orange juice.

105
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:28,000
Unfortunately, oranges are seasonal and perishable.

106
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:32,280
But they are also a rich and excellent source of vitamin C.

107
00:09:33,480 --> 00:09:40,740
As World War II broke out, there was big concern about the age-old disease of scurvy caused by the lack of vitamin C.

108
00:09:40,741 --> 00:09:46,820
The U.S. government appealed to the Florida Citrus Commission to find a solution.

109
00:09:48,680 --> 00:09:52,040
Donald Atkins, Louis Gardner McDowell, and Edwin Moore

110
00:09:52,041 --> 00:09:57,560
searched for a way to get the essential nutrients to the soldiers and civilians who needed it overseas.

111
00:09:58,980 --> 00:10:02,860
The scientists wanted to create a good-tasting product that wouldn't spoil.

112
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:08,200
Near the war's end, they perfected frozen concentrate orange juice.

113
00:10:08,201 --> 00:10:08,901
Dr.

114
00:10:08,902 --> 00:10:16,200
Moore and his associates reduced the water content in juice by applying low heat in a high vacuum.

115
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:19,860
They then added fresh juice to the concentrate for taste.

116
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:22,140
Finally, they froze the mixture.

117
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:29,080
When Moore reconstituted the frozen concentrate with water, he thought it tasted like fresh juice.

118
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:32,520
But the true test came at the yearly Orange Festival.

119
00:10:32,521 --> 00:10:43,900
Atkins and I made concentrate and the Citrus Commission had a booth at the Florida Orange

120
00:10:43,901 --> 00:10:50,700
Festival and we were demonstrating how it was made, how it was reconstituted, it had the vitamin

121
00:10:50,701 --> 00:10:52,010
content and all.

122
00:10:52,011 --> 00:10:58,480
And we had one of these books out to sign, you know, with the comments. And the

123
00:10:58,481 --> 00:11:03,760
The comments of 5,000 people was, this tastes just like fresh juice.

124
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:07,260
When will we be able to buy the product?

125
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:10,300
So right then we knew we had something.

126
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:13,840
Almost overnight, a new industry was born.

127
00:11:15,020 --> 00:11:16,880
Farmers rushed to plant orange groves.

128
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:23,220
In the 1950s, orange juice became available on breakfast tables year-round.

129
00:11:23,221 --> 00:11:30,220
Florida produces more than 1.5 billion gallons of orange juice a year.

130
00:11:30,221 --> 00:11:37,220
In a rush to get somewhere?

131
00:11:37,221 --> 00:11:43,220
Next, see how war got the wheels of progress rolling and altered transportation forever.

132
00:11:43,221 --> 00:11:52,220
American Ezra Warner invented the tin can opener in 1858.

133
00:11:52,221 --> 00:12:00,480
Nearly 50 years after the invention of the tin can, Inventions of War will return on Modern Marvels.

134
00:12:01,980 --> 00:12:06,360
We now return to Inventions of War on Modern Marvels.

135
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:15,000
Among the many innovations of war, few have rolled into civilian life as smoothly as those involving transportation.

136
00:12:15,880 --> 00:12:21,660
With the luxuries of CD player and air conditioning, the Jeep is at home, off-road or in town.

137
00:12:22,220 --> 00:12:29,420
The ancestor of today's Jeep Wrangler was a true workhorse and decorated war hero of World War II.

138
00:12:31,420 --> 00:12:34,340
General Eisenhower, at the end of the war, summarized it.

139
00:12:34,440 --> 00:12:36,340
He said there were three weapons that were decisive.

140
00:12:36,920 --> 00:12:37,980
The Jeep was one of them.

141
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:41,076
On the eve of World War II, the U.S.

142
00:12:41,077 --> 00:12:44,220
government, desperate for a general purpose vehicle,

143
00:12:44,900 --> 00:12:47,220
requested submissions from various car companies.

144
00:12:47,221 --> 00:12:59,340
The government chose the American Bantam Car Company's design, a lightweight 60-horsepower, four-wheel drive vehicle with a top speed of 65 miles per hour.

145
00:13:08,940 --> 00:13:12,260
After grueling tests, the Army fell in love.

146
00:13:12,261 --> 00:13:17,260
Soldiers first shortened general purpose to GP or Jeep.

147
00:13:17,261 --> 00:13:22,260
The name stuck, but the vehicles rarely did.

148
00:13:22,261 --> 00:13:28,260
The demand was so high that the government asked other companies to produce Jeeps.

149
00:13:28,261 --> 00:13:34,260
It enabled our troops to go mechanized anywhere that the enemy was.

150
00:13:34,261 --> 00:13:36,260
We didn't have to rely on foot power.

151
00:13:36,261 --> 00:13:41,260
The Jeep was used as a reconnaissance vehicle in the desert and elsewhere.

152
00:13:41,261 --> 00:13:43,240
It was armed and used as an assault vehicle.

153
00:13:43,800 --> 00:13:45,880
It was used for cargo. It was used for medevac.

154
00:13:46,220 --> 00:13:48,180
There were more than 400,000 of them made.

155
00:13:51,860 --> 00:13:55,100
The Jeep became a much-loved symbol of World War II.

156
00:13:58,860 --> 00:14:02,080
After the war, Jeep manufacturers continued making them.

157
00:14:02,840 --> 00:14:06,540
Today, Daimler Chrysler sells 84,000 Jeeps a year.

158
00:14:06,541 --> 00:14:14,880
During war, a country's natural resources are among its greatest assets.

159
00:14:16,300 --> 00:14:21,120
The United States is fortunate with abundant reserves, from steel to petroleum.

160
00:14:22,180 --> 00:14:24,847
But at the beginning of World War II, the U.S.

161
00:14:24,848 --> 00:14:27,920
was caught off guard by one severe resource shortage.

162
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:33,800
A resource without which the U.S. would have no hope of winning the war.

163
00:14:33,801 --> 00:14:34,629
Robert R.

164
00:14:34,630 --> 00:14:43,660
Rubber was, after steel, probably the most important strategic material for fighting World War II.

165
00:14:44,180 --> 00:14:48,840
And the reason for that was that it was the first truly mechanized war.

166
00:14:49,180 --> 00:14:52,880
Machines had to be mobile. It was the most mobile war in history.

167
00:14:53,820 --> 00:15:01,440
In the 1930s, aware of Germany's limited natural resources, Hitler made developing synthetic rubber a top priority.

168
00:15:02,440 --> 00:15:06,760
German scientists created a petroleum-based synthetic rubber called Buna.

169
00:15:08,940 --> 00:15:09,640
The U.S.

170
00:15:09,641 --> 00:15:15,420
military, confident their supply of natural rubber from Singapore was secure, delayed development.

171
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:22,100
Two months after Pearl Harbor, however, the Japanese captured Singapore.

172
00:15:23,300 --> 00:15:27,580
The sudden loss forced the nation into its first real resource emergency.

173
00:15:27,581 --> 00:15:33,300
Here are two simple rules for this rubber emergency.

174
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:37,700
First, turn in all the old rubber.

175
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:41,400
Secondly, cut the use of your car.

176
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:46,260
Tires, they're humble items, but without them, trucks don't roll,

177
00:15:46,360 --> 00:15:50,720
and if trucks don't roll, the tanks don't have gas, and the soldiers don't have food.

178
00:15:52,420 --> 00:15:55,380
Rubber recycling alone couldn't keep the war machine rolling.

179
00:15:56,240 --> 00:15:59,200
By 1942, the U.S. was desperate for rubber.

180
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:04,840
Former rivals, professors and chemists, banded together to work on a solution.

181
00:16:06,200 --> 00:16:11,320
Soon, scientists found a synthetic rubber chemical concoction suitable for molding into tires.

182
00:16:12,980 --> 00:16:16,860
The government rushed to construct 50 synthetic rubber plants.

183
00:16:18,260 --> 00:16:20,200
Tire production soared.

184
00:16:20,201 --> 00:16:26,540
In 1944, plants generated more than 800,000 tons of synthetic rubber,

185
00:16:27,040 --> 00:16:30,980
far more tires than they were producing before the war from natural rubber,

186
00:16:31,980 --> 00:16:34,300
inflating America's confidence once again.

187
00:16:36,640 --> 00:16:40,800
After the war, tire companies continued manufacturing synthetic rubber.

188
00:16:41,980 --> 00:16:48,580
Today, American tire companies combine 60% synthetic and 40% natural rubber to make tires.

189
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:56,480
While working on the rubber problem, an American chemist came up with a truly silly solution.

190
00:16:57,360 --> 00:17:03,480
A contract was given to GE to try to come up with its own form of synthetic rubber, if they could.

191
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:11,560
And GE assigned one of its top polymer scientists by the name of James Wright to work on the synthetic rubber problem.

192
00:17:11,561 --> 00:17:17,440
They came up with a very rubber-like compound, which they called Nutty Putty,

193
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:20,820
but it had none of the resilience of a true synthetic rubber,

194
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:23,220
and GE used it as a novelty.

195
00:17:23,760 --> 00:17:27,780
It was given out as kind of gifts among GE executives.

196
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:29,260
It was kind of a joke.

197
00:17:30,900 --> 00:17:34,860
But GE chemists loved taking Nutty Putty to cocktail parties.

198
00:17:35,620 --> 00:17:39,640
At one particular event, Ruth Falgader, a toy store owner,

199
00:17:39,641 --> 00:17:42,620
noticed the fun people were having with the pink blob

200
00:17:42,621 --> 00:17:45,880
and decided to include it in her holiday toy catalog.

201
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:50,000
That season, the novelty sold well,

202
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:52,080
but she didn't see a future in it.

203
00:17:52,460 --> 00:17:54,200
However, one of her associates did.

204
00:17:54,220 --> 00:17:54,920
What is Silly Putty?

205
00:17:55,120 --> 00:17:58,100
Peter Hodgson bought a large batch of the goo from GE,

206
00:17:58,940 --> 00:18:00,600
packaged hand-sized globs,

207
00:18:00,860 --> 00:18:02,900
and dubbed it Silly Putty.

208
00:18:03,760 --> 00:18:06,340
And he came up with the brilliant notion

209
00:18:06,341 --> 00:18:10,040
of the plastic egg to put the silly putty in,

210
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:13,180
and the rest is novelty history.

211
00:18:14,920 --> 00:18:17,700
Binney and Smith, the makers of silly putty,

212
00:18:17,920 --> 00:18:21,700
have sold more than 300 million of the eggs since 1950.

213
00:18:21,701 --> 00:18:35,701
Over 1.6 million people travel by plane in the U.S. every day.

214
00:18:37,820 --> 00:18:41,100
The turbojet engine revolutionized the way we travel,

215
00:18:41,560 --> 00:18:45,800
reducing the duration of a transatlantic crossing to a couple of cocktails in a movie.

216
00:18:47,100 --> 00:18:50,100
But coach seating and airline meals wouldn't be the same today

217
00:18:50,101 --> 00:18:53,300
without the research done in the shadow of an impending war.

218
00:18:56,920 --> 00:19:01,880
Both Germany and England simultaneously worked on the jet engine before World War II.

219
00:19:05,080 --> 00:19:08,800
Turbo jet engines worked by compressing air in the firing chamber,

220
00:19:09,560 --> 00:19:12,340
injecting atomized fuel and igniting the mixture,

221
00:19:13,140 --> 00:19:14,700
propelling the vehicle forward.

222
00:19:14,701 --> 00:19:27,220
In August 1939, Germany got their jet program off the ground with the HE-178, the world's first jet aircraft.

223
00:19:29,300 --> 00:19:33,880
The far superior ME-262 fighter jet soon followed.

224
00:19:35,160 --> 00:19:36,450
It was a world beater.

225
00:19:36,451 --> 00:19:42,140
It would go almost 600 miles an hour at a time when propeller aircraft were doing well to do 450.

226
00:19:42,141 --> 00:19:48,400
Nothing could stop it except Adolf Hitler, and I think we're all fortunate that Hitler was a meddler.

227
00:19:48,720 --> 00:19:50,020
He thought it would make a great bomber.

228
00:19:50,600 --> 00:19:56,200
So he instructed the Luftwaffe to hang two bombs under it, which slowed it down to about 400 miles an hour,

229
00:19:56,620 --> 00:19:59,420
under the top speed of our fighter so we could catch it.

230
00:20:00,080 --> 00:20:05,680
By the time they got around to saying, yes, let's use it as a fighter, it was 1945 and the war was lost.

231
00:20:06,540 --> 00:20:08,685
After the war, England and the U.S.

232
00:20:08,686 --> 00:20:12,240
continued developing military and commercial jet aircraft.

233
00:20:13,540 --> 00:20:19,460
The British introduced the first commercial jet airliner, the de Havilland Comet, in 1952.

234
00:20:21,860 --> 00:20:25,720
Boeing's answer to commercial jet transportation was the 707.

235
00:20:25,721 --> 00:20:39,721
Now the Boeing 707, which became the 737 and led to all the other ones, was basically a rebuilt Boeing B-47 bomber that was developed in the late 40s to be our first

236
00:20:41,581 --> 00:20:43,600
strategic jet bomber.

237
00:20:43,601 --> 00:20:50,080
The way the engines were hung off the wings, the tail grouping, the pressurization to keep cockpit pressure,

238
00:20:50,680 --> 00:20:58,140
all those things were pioneered on the B-47, enhanced in the B-52, which came very shortly thereafter,

239
00:20:58,780 --> 00:21:02,500
and then led immediately to the 707.

240
00:21:07,700 --> 00:21:10,040
War did more than shorten distances.

241
00:21:10,041 --> 00:21:13,140
In some ways, it brought us closer together.

242
00:21:14,140 --> 00:21:20,040
In other ways, it propelled us farther away, taking us places we've never been before.

243
00:21:21,780 --> 00:21:29,940
Military engineers predict the use of the B-52 bomber will extend beyond 2045, a lifespan of more than 100 years.

244
00:21:30,580 --> 00:21:31,800
Modern Marvels will return.

245
00:21:33,320 --> 00:21:37,540
We now return to Inventions of War on Modern Marvels.

246
00:21:40,040 --> 00:21:54,040
Some inventions of war have found everyday applications that are sometimes seen as a blessing and a curse, like radar.

247
00:21:57,040 --> 00:22:04,040
In the first part of the 20th century, researchers discovered they could detect flying objects with electromagnetic waves.

248
00:22:04,041 --> 00:22:11,040
By transmitting radio signals, metallic objects reflected the signals back to the transmitter.

249
00:22:11,041 --> 00:22:17,040
Engineers called the invention, radar, for radio detection and ranging.

250
00:22:17,041 --> 00:22:22,040
Serious radar work began in the 1930s.

251
00:22:22,041 --> 00:22:27,040
The British, fearing a future air war, put their top engineers on it.

252
00:22:27,041 --> 00:22:33,040
By 1938, England had the world's first practical radar system.

253
00:22:33,041 --> 00:22:38,660
The possession of an effective radar chain in 1940

254
00:22:38,661 --> 00:22:48,040
was probably the single most important technological advantage that the British had.

255
00:22:49,420 --> 00:22:52,300
England's pre-war anxieties were prophetic.

256
00:22:53,900 --> 00:22:56,900
Between August 1940 and May 1941,

257
00:22:57,880 --> 00:23:01,520
the Luftwaffe pummeled England with bombs during the Battle of Britain.

258
00:23:01,521 --> 00:23:07,840
What the radar chain did was give them the crucial advance warning.

259
00:23:08,760 --> 00:23:13,020
By 1940, the radar chain could look into northern France,

260
00:23:13,160 --> 00:23:17,180
and you could see the German raids forming up over northern France,

261
00:23:17,700 --> 00:23:21,700
and you could deduce their strength, their direction,

262
00:23:22,300 --> 00:23:28,580
and you could employ the fighters who would always be outnumbered in the most advantageous way.

263
00:23:28,581 --> 00:23:33,580
Radar research led to another invention that we see as indispensable today.

264
00:23:34,580 --> 00:23:39,580
During the war, a British radar researcher created the cavity magnetron.

265
00:23:40,580 --> 00:23:46,580
The device generated shorter waves, or microwaves, that made radar much more accurate.

266
00:23:47,580 --> 00:23:51,580
Carefully transported to the United States for large-scale duplication,

267
00:23:51,581 --> 00:23:53,432
scale duplication, Percy L.

268
00:23:53,433 --> 00:23:57,960
Spencer, a Raytheon engineer, worked to further improve the cavity

269
00:23:57,961 --> 00:23:58,919
magnetron.

270
00:23:58,920 --> 00:24:06,780
One day in 1945, Spencer was standing in front of a magnetron tube when he noticed

271
00:24:06,781 --> 00:24:10,806
that the candy bar in his pocket inexplicably had melted.

272
00:24:10,807 --> 00:24:13,420
Wondering if the microwaves generated

273
00:24:13,421 --> 00:24:18,728
by the magnetron did the cooking, he placed kernels of popcorn beside it.

274
00:24:18,729 --> 00:24:19,600
They popped,

275
00:24:19,601 --> 00:24:22,540
and leftovers would never be the same.

276
00:24:24,800 --> 00:24:29,700
In 1947, Raytheon demonstrated the Radar Range,

277
00:24:29,940 --> 00:24:31,680
the world's first microwave oven.

278
00:24:32,340 --> 00:24:33,840
It was the size of a refrigerator

279
00:24:33,841 --> 00:24:36,400
and cost between $2,000 and $3,000.

280
00:24:39,560 --> 00:24:42,060
Many credit radar is saving England,

281
00:24:42,560 --> 00:24:44,160
as well as a few inept cooks.

282
00:24:45,160 --> 00:24:49,160
But the World War II invention is still helping save lives.

283
00:24:49,600 --> 00:24:52,860
by keeping track of air traffic and watching the weather.

284
00:24:53,760 --> 00:24:55,600
It's even mapping the surface of Venus.

285
00:24:58,680 --> 00:25:02,040
Although some war innovations have a tendency to bring us down,

286
00:25:02,640 --> 00:25:05,280
others lift us higher than we've ever gone before.

287
00:25:06,280 --> 00:25:10,740
Five, four, three, two, one.

288
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:13,740
Liftoff. We have a liftoff.

289
00:25:13,860 --> 00:25:15,720
32 minutes past the hour.

290
00:25:15,721 --> 00:25:23,060
A rocket is a means of delivering a payload to a specific destination, whether that's

291
00:25:23,061 --> 00:25:27,980
the moon or the Soviet Union.

292
00:25:27,981 --> 00:25:32,220
Rockets trace their origins to the experiments of Dr. Robert Goddard.

293
00:25:32,221 --> 00:25:38,440
The U.S. scientist launched the first liquid-propelled rocket in 1926.

294
00:25:38,441 --> 00:25:46,520
Independently, a group of rocket enthusiasts began similar work in Germany.

295
00:25:46,521 --> 00:25:50,220
Germany in the 1930s was a volatile place.

296
00:25:50,221 --> 00:25:56,640
Many Germans felt the Treaty of Versailles signed at the end of World War I was unfair.

297
00:25:56,641 --> 00:26:02,160
It limited the country's military and blamed Germany for causing World War I.

298
00:26:02,161 --> 00:26:08,080
Adolf Hitler rose to power by playing on the German people's feelings.

299
00:26:08,081 --> 00:26:11,320
the German military soon showed interest in the rocket group's work.

300
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:13,720
This was, in effect, a loophole.

301
00:26:14,160 --> 00:26:17,180
There was no direct ban on rocket research

302
00:26:17,181 --> 00:26:19,940
because, of course, the people that drew up the Versailles Treaty

303
00:26:19,941 --> 00:26:23,540
knew nothing about rockets and didn't anticipate rockets becoming a weapon.

304
00:26:24,920 --> 00:26:26,180
Realizing the potential,

305
00:26:26,860 --> 00:26:29,560
the military constructed a rocket testing facility

306
00:26:29,561 --> 00:26:32,680
and made Werner von Braun project director.

307
00:26:34,120 --> 00:26:37,380
Von Braun's team developed the most impressive rocket of its day,

308
00:26:37,381 --> 00:26:44,840
the V2. The V2 burned alcohol and liquid oxygen fuel and carried a one-ton warhead.

309
00:26:47,500 --> 00:26:54,860
Germany terrorized England by launching more than 1,100 V2s at London, killing 10,000 people.

310
00:26:56,020 --> 00:26:59,900
Fortunately for the Allies, it was too late to turn the tide of the war.

311
00:27:00,900 --> 00:27:04,680
After the war, we and the Russians both captured a large number of these

312
00:27:04,681 --> 00:27:10,620
rockets and use them for initial tests not just for military purposes but for

313
00:27:10,621 --> 00:27:16,580
launching satellites and high-altitude probes we kept refining the design

314
00:27:16,581 --> 00:27:24,360
improving it the moon rockets were basically really overgrown v2s

315
00:27:24,361 --> 00:27:33,780
Germans innovations led to the space race for both war rockets ICBMs and for

316
00:27:33,781 --> 00:27:37,780
satellite launchers and initially they were the same thing.

317
00:27:37,781 --> 00:27:41,780
No war invention changed the world more

318
00:27:41,781 --> 00:27:45,780
than the atomic bomb. Harnessing

319
00:27:45,781 --> 00:27:49,780
the same power that destroyed Hiroshima, nuclear energy

320
00:27:49,781 --> 00:27:53,780
provides a significant portion of the world's power needs today.

321
00:27:53,781 --> 00:27:57,780
Despite the fact that commercial nuclear power

322
00:27:57,781 --> 00:28:01,780
is derived from the research leading to the bombs that were dropped on Hiroshima and

323
00:28:01,781 --> 00:28:06,780
there's really very little connection between nuclear power plants and bombs.

324
00:28:06,781 --> 00:28:13,780
Nuclear power, I consider to be a very attractive way to produce electricity commercially

325
00:28:13,781 --> 00:28:20,780
because it's so clean, it is an extremely safe technology.

326
00:28:21,780 --> 00:28:26,780
Most modern nuclear energy plants generate power by uranium fission.

327
00:28:26,781 --> 00:28:35,780
A chain reaction occurs when a neutron is fired into a uranium-235 atom and splits it.

328
00:28:35,781 --> 00:28:40,780
At the same time, this releases other neutrons that split other uranium atoms,

329
00:28:40,781 --> 00:28:44,780
generating a great amount of thermal energy.

330
00:28:44,781 --> 00:28:52,780
In the core, the heat creates steam, which turns traditional turbine generators, producing electricity.

331
00:28:52,781 --> 00:28:56,900
The history of nuclear power dates back to the Second World War.

332
00:28:57,860 --> 00:29:05,660
Physicist Enrico Fermi initiated the first sustained nuclear reaction at the University of Chicago in December of 1942.

333
00:29:08,340 --> 00:29:16,320
Shortly thereafter, Fermi and a number of the very prestigious members of his team began having a series of meetings,

334
00:29:16,321 --> 00:29:28,080
some of which were directed to determining how this new concept could be applied to the generation of electricity for civilian commercial needs.

335
00:29:28,820 --> 00:29:35,020
In some sense, the groundwork of the nuclear power system that we have now in this country was laid at that time.

336
00:29:36,400 --> 00:29:39,520
After the war, atomic research continued.

337
00:29:40,340 --> 00:29:42,940
Scientists searched for a peaceful use of the atom.

338
00:29:42,941 --> 00:29:48,560
And in 1958, America's first commercial nuclear power plant powered up.

339
00:29:50,860 --> 00:29:52,331
Currently, the U.S.

340
00:29:52,332 --> 00:29:57,440
operates 104 nuclear plants, generating 20% of the nation's power.

341
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:05,340
The fissioning of one pound of uranium is roughly the equivalent of burning 3 million pounds of coal.

342
00:30:06,760 --> 00:30:10,420
Of course, there are also negative aspects of nuclear power.

343
00:30:11,180 --> 00:30:16,340
Although it is sometimes reprocessed, spent uranium fuel is highly radioactive

344
00:30:16,341 --> 00:30:19,760
and remains so for thousands of years.

345
00:30:21,020 --> 00:30:23,560
People view this war invention differently.

346
00:30:24,140 --> 00:30:25,740
A catastrophe waiting to happen,

347
00:30:26,300 --> 00:30:29,940
or a viable solution to diminishing fossil fuel reserves.

348
00:30:31,880 --> 00:30:35,880
In the course of your day, some war inventions help us cope

349
00:30:35,881 --> 00:30:38,480
with a dangerous world away from the battlefield.

350
00:30:40,420 --> 00:30:41,420
Ow!

351
00:30:41,421 --> 00:30:42,420
Yeah, no, I heard it.

352
00:30:42,421 --> 00:30:43,420
I'm not hurting.

353
00:30:43,421 --> 00:30:48,420
Next, war inventions designed to save lives.

354
00:30:48,421 --> 00:30:53,420
Medicines, miracle cures, and magic bullets.

355
00:30:55,420 --> 00:31:00,420
Eighty-four percent of American households own a microwave oven.

356
00:31:00,421 --> 00:31:05,420
Inventions of War will return on Modern Marvels.

357
00:31:05,421 --> 00:31:10,420
We now return to Inventions of War on Modern Marvels.

358
00:31:12,420 --> 00:31:18,420
One of man's objectives in war is to kill the enemy and save as many of his own troops as possible.

359
00:31:21,420 --> 00:31:26,420
From the horrific wounds and disease of war have come major medical advancements.

360
00:31:29,420 --> 00:31:34,420
Often overlooked are the medical advancements made during the deadliest war in American history.

361
00:31:35,420 --> 00:31:39,920
A war that cost more American lives than both world wars combined.

362
00:31:41,240 --> 00:31:47,440
Major seeds of modern medical practice began in the Civil War and have continued to develop at a rapid state of a sense.

363
00:31:47,680 --> 00:31:52,500
For example, the creation of an ambulance system may have been the greatest advancement of all.

364
00:31:52,820 --> 00:31:56,940
How to get a wounded man from the battlefield to a first aid station.

365
00:31:57,220 --> 00:32:01,540
It seemed so logical to us. It didn't exist at that time.

366
00:32:01,541 --> 00:32:08,400
At the beginning of the Civil War, there were many problems with evacuating injured soldiers from the battlefield.

367
00:32:10,020 --> 00:32:17,100
The soldiers who were assigned to operate the rickety ambulance carts were often those who were not suited or willing to fight.

368
00:32:19,700 --> 00:32:22,080
Devastating battles left thousands injured.

369
00:32:23,600 --> 00:32:27,060
Those who couldn't move might lay for days before help arrived.

370
00:32:27,780 --> 00:32:29,340
Often, that was too late.

371
00:32:29,341 --> 00:32:37,160
Identifying the problem, Jonathan Letterman, a medical director with the Union Army, established

372
00:32:37,161 --> 00:32:39,760
an ambulance corps.

373
00:32:39,761 --> 00:32:44,980
Under his system, soldiers were assigned to systematically collect the wounded and transport

374
00:32:44,981 --> 00:32:47,460
them to field hospitals.

375
00:32:47,461 --> 00:32:52,620
The ambulances also carried a prescribed list of medical supplies.

376
00:32:52,621 --> 00:32:59,620
After the war, civilian hospitals adopted Letterman's system.

377
00:33:00,380 --> 00:33:03,728
The crisis of war provides a proving ground.

378
00:33:03,729 --> 00:33:07,380
A lab where new drugs and techniques are tested.

379
00:33:07,560 --> 00:33:12,860
Many times on the battlefield, bullets aren't what kill people.

380
00:33:12,861 --> 00:33:20,580
In all wars through the 19th century, it was expected that two or three times the number

381
00:33:20,581 --> 00:33:25,580
of people would die of disease than died of enemy action.

382
00:33:25,581 --> 00:33:31,640
One of the big killers was typhoid fever.

383
00:33:31,641 --> 00:33:36,800
The disease caused by the Salmonella typhi bacterium and transmitted through contaminated

384
00:33:36,801 --> 00:33:42,340
food or water was fatal to 30% of the people who contracted it.

385
00:33:42,341 --> 00:33:50,380
In the late 1800s, English bacteriologist Almroth Wright discovered a vaccine to prevent typhoid.

386
00:33:51,340 --> 00:33:55,620
Wright proposed mandatory inoculation of all soldiers fighting in the Boer War.

387
00:33:57,140 --> 00:34:01,300
Not trusting the science, many objected and refused vaccination.

388
00:34:02,220 --> 00:34:05,400
Only 14,000 troops received the typhoid vaccine.

389
00:34:06,120 --> 00:34:09,360
The results were catastrophic for those who refused vaccination.

390
00:34:10,220 --> 00:34:15,440
One out of every six soldiers developed the disease, and 9,000 died.

391
00:34:19,660 --> 00:34:27,540
In 1898, the United States went to war with Spain, and there was a rapid mobilization of volunteers.

392
00:34:28,660 --> 00:34:34,120
In the training camps of that war, typhoid fever epidemics broke out.

393
00:34:35,180 --> 00:34:39,120
Hundreds died, thousands were sick, before they ever got to the enemy.

394
00:34:39,360 --> 00:34:42,180
People are not supposed to die before they get to the enemy.

395
00:34:42,580 --> 00:34:44,620
The U.S. learned from the English.

396
00:34:45,480 --> 00:34:48,400
The U.S. Army developed their own typhoid fever vaccine.

397
00:34:49,220 --> 00:34:53,860
In 1911, immunization against typhoid fever became standard policy

398
00:34:53,861 --> 00:34:57,680
and is only the first of many of the constructed immunizations

399
00:34:57,681 --> 00:35:01,880
that we use to protect soldiers, sailors, airmen, and Marines to this day.

400
00:35:01,881 --> 00:35:08,880
By World War I, the incidence of typhoid dropped from 10% to 2%.

401
00:35:08,881 --> 00:35:22,620
During World War II, the battle against disease-causing microorganisms saw an innovation that has saved more sick and injured than any single invention in history.

402
00:35:23,580 --> 00:35:26,380
Penicillin, the magic bullet.

403
00:35:28,040 --> 00:35:36,440
From ancient times, doctors and patients had dreamed of a drug that would make people well, almost miraculously.

404
00:35:36,441 --> 00:35:49,060
In the 1920s, Alexander Fleming serendipitously discovered that the mold penicillin produced

405
00:35:49,061 --> 00:35:56,160
a juice that would kill other organisms, antibiosis, anti-life.

406
00:35:59,280 --> 00:36:03,800
Fleming published his work, but was never able to purify penicillin nor produce it in

407
00:36:03,801 --> 00:36:04,501
mass quantities.

408
00:36:05,480 --> 00:36:07,440
It was forgotten for a decade.

409
00:36:09,840 --> 00:36:15,020
World War II, however, provided new incentive for finding a drug that cured infection.

410
00:36:16,960 --> 00:36:23,540
Scientists Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, working in England, continued Fleming's research.

411
00:36:24,780 --> 00:36:28,100
They came up with a purified penicillin injection.

412
00:36:29,600 --> 00:36:33,260
Limited human testing hinted at the miraculous effects of penicillin.

413
00:36:33,261 --> 00:36:38,340
But the war prevented them from producing enough of it to benefit many people.

414
00:36:39,440 --> 00:36:43,440
With the fate of millions in their hands, they turned to America.

415
00:36:44,580 --> 00:36:48,680
Ernst Chain secretly carried the miracle mold to the United States.

416
00:36:49,680 --> 00:37:00,300
It was almost the equivalent of the Manhattan Project in the effort to get penicillin in sufficient quantities to take care of all the soldiers wounded.

417
00:37:00,301 --> 00:37:07,180
the National Research Council convened pharmaceutical executives and said,

418
00:37:07,280 --> 00:37:08,654
we have a problem.

419
00:37:08,655 --> 00:37:13,920
We need you to cooperate, share your data, and let's figure out a way

420
00:37:13,921 --> 00:37:20,780
to make this drug, this magic bullet, available now.

421
00:37:21,700 --> 00:37:24,940
After an exhaustive search to find the perfect mold,

422
00:37:25,640 --> 00:37:29,340
scientists discovered a method to produce penicillin in larger quantities.

423
00:37:30,300 --> 00:37:33,160
Thousands and later millions benefited.

424
00:37:34,020 --> 00:37:37,420
America is continually increasing its output of penicillin,

425
00:37:37,820 --> 00:37:40,440
a new drug that affects almost miraculous cures.

426
00:37:41,600 --> 00:37:45,620
By the end of the war, the magic bullet has been found.

427
00:37:46,400 --> 00:37:51,140
An infectious bacterial disease looks like it's been conquered.

428
00:37:52,820 --> 00:37:57,080
Next, see what innovation sprang from the mere threat of war.

429
00:37:57,081 --> 00:38:00,100
Hot products from a cold war.

430
00:38:00,300 --> 00:38:06,300
In 1943, the United States produced 28 pounds of penicillin.

431
00:38:06,301 --> 00:38:12,300
In 1945, production increased to 14,000 pounds.

432
00:38:12,301 --> 00:38:16,300
Inventions of War will return on Modern Marvels.

433
00:38:16,301 --> 00:38:23,300
While war is about tearing the enemy apart, it has connected others.

434
00:38:23,301 --> 00:38:31,440
Even desktop computers trace their origins to the giant machines developed during World War II.

435
00:38:32,320 --> 00:38:37,420
The U.S. built ENIAC to quickly calculate Canon trajectory charts.

436
00:38:38,540 --> 00:38:45,640
The monstrous computer utilized 18,000 vacuum tubes and generated an enormous amount of heat.

437
00:38:46,320 --> 00:38:52,940
Although it took up 1,500 square feet, it could only operate for a few minutes before blowing a vacuum tube.

438
00:38:53,300 --> 00:38:57,900
It also had less computing power than a modern handheld computer.

439
00:39:02,040 --> 00:39:04,780
The heat of battle has always bred innovation,

440
00:39:05,260 --> 00:39:08,660
but it was a colder environment that fueled major advancements

441
00:39:08,661 --> 00:39:10,860
during the second half of the 20th century.

442
00:39:12,620 --> 00:39:17,160
The Cold War ignited an arms race and a race for space.

443
00:39:17,680 --> 00:39:21,220
The United States and the Soviet Union spent trillions of dollars

444
00:39:21,221 --> 00:39:22,360
to stay ahead of the other.

445
00:39:23,300 --> 00:39:30,280
The Cold War really began seriously when the Soviets exploded their first nuclear weapon.

446
00:39:31,080 --> 00:39:34,380
And then just a year after us, exploded their first hydrogen weapon.

447
00:39:36,040 --> 00:39:39,940
As the stakes got bigger, technology got smaller.

448
00:39:41,120 --> 00:39:46,020
We went from the tube technology to the transistor technology to the chip technology,

449
00:39:46,220 --> 00:39:51,640
all driven by the military's need to shrink things down, reduce their weight, and improve their reliability.

450
00:39:51,641 --> 00:40:03,080
In 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik, the world's first artificial satellite, and lifted the competition between superpowers to a new level.

451
00:40:03,920 --> 00:40:08,300
It was a milestone for the Soviets and a wake-up call for the United States.

452
00:40:09,160 --> 00:40:13,300
Americans were left feeling U.S. technology lagged behind that of the Russians.

453
00:40:14,200 --> 00:40:17,000
The Soviet launching of Earth satellites is an achievement.

454
00:40:17,001 --> 00:40:27,900
In response, President Dwight Eisenhower established the Advanced Research Projects Agency, ARPA, to stimulate military science and technology research.

455
00:40:29,780 --> 00:40:37,320
During the 1960s, ARPA worked with many institutions, each using different computer systems and software.

456
00:40:38,240 --> 00:40:41,140
The agency started working on a way to connect everyone.

457
00:40:41,141 --> 00:40:48,140
ARPA later became DARPA when the word defense was added to the name.

458
00:40:48,141 --> 00:40:54,140
DARPA essentially committed to build a very small scale network for research purposes.

459
00:40:54,141 --> 00:40:59,140
The motivation was actually efficiency within the DARPA research community,

